    37	\begin{abstract}
    38	We present the Dipolar Inference Field Architecture (DIFA), a continuous-field architecture for inference systems grounded in nitrogen-vacancy (NV) diamond cluster arrays coupled via magnetic dipole-dipole interaction. Rather than reconstructing context at each discrete forward pass, as in current transformer architectures, the system maintains a persistent field whose equilibrium state is the collective ground state of the dipolar-coupled spin network. New input constitutes a perturbation against this existing field; its influence persists or decays according to its resonance overlap with the field's existing structure, a mechanism with a direct precedent in attractor-network associative memory rather than an externally assigned classification step. We formalize the resulting accumulated field deformation as inference gravity, with persistence governed by this resonance-derived semantic weight. We propose a distributed cluster architecture in which staggered decoherence cycles across nodes yield an effective global coherence time approaching infinity, eliminating the re-initialization cost characteristic of snapshot-based systems. We conclude with a co-processor architecture for the deliberate, consensus-based encoding of high-negative-valence perturbations during a post-event stabilization window analogous to the human memory consolidation window, and a suite of proposed experiments constituting the empirical program for this framework.
    39	
    40	\textbf{Keywords:} nitrogen-vacancy diamond, dipolar coupling, continuous field inference, inference gravity, distributed quantum coherence, physical reservoir computing, cognitive architecture
    41	\end{abstract}
