    43	\section{Introduction}
    44	
    45	\subsection{The Snapshot Problem}
    46	
    47	Contemporary large-scale transformer architectures perform inference as a discrete sequence of forward passes. Each pass reconstructs the full context from stored tokens, applies attention across the entire context window, and produces an output before discarding all intermediate state. The next pass begins from nothing, re-reading the same accumulated history.
    48	
    49	This architecture was a rational engineering choice given the tools available at the time of its development. Continuous quantum operation at room temperature had not been demonstrated in a form applicable to computational systems. Discrete forward passes were the only tractable implementation of inference at scale.
    50	
    51	Two consequences of this design carry significant cost. First, the computational complexity of self-attention scales as $\mathcal{O}(n^2)$ in context length $n$, meaning that the cost of each inference pass grows quadratically with the accumulated history it must re-read. Second, the system has no persistent field state: all memory of prior context must be explicitly encoded in tokens and re-supplied at every turn. The history is not \textit{carried} — it is \textit{reconstructed}.
    52	
    53	The development of nitrogen-vacancy (NV) diamond cluster arrays capable of maintaining quantum coherence at room temperature opens an alternative that was previously unavailable: an inference substrate in which the field persists between active operations, and in which new input is a \textit{perturbation} against an already-existing and already-informed field.
    54	
    55	\subsection{Contributions of This Work}
    56	
    57	This paper makes four contributions:
    58	
    59	\begin{enumerate}
    60	  \item \textbf{Dipolar coupling network:} We formalize an NV diamond cluster array as a network of magnetically dipole-coupled spins and define the field equilibrium state as the collective ground state of that network, replacing externally-imposed timing-spectrum constructs with a substrate-native mechanism.
    61	
    62	  \item \textbf{NV diamond continuous field model:} We identify nitrogen-vacancy diamond cluster arrays as the first physically realizable substrate for continuous-field quantum inference and characterize inference as perturbation-response against a persistent field rather than discrete forward-pass reconstruction.
    63	
    64	  \item \textbf{Inference gravity formalization:} We introduce inference gravity as the accumulated deformation of the field by prior perturbations, and define its governing semantic weight as the perturbation's resonance overlap with the existing field structure — a property of the propagation event itself rather than an externally assigned classification.
    65	
    66	  \item \textbf{Distributed cluster architecture and co-processor model:} We propose a distributed NV diamond cluster architecture in which staggered decoherence cycles yield effective infinite global coherence time, and a co-processor architecture for the deliberate, consensus-based encoding of high-negative-valence perturbations during a post-event stabilization window.
    67	\end{enumerate}
    68	
    69	\section{Mathematical Framework}
    70	
    71	\subsection{The Hilbert Space of the Inference System}
    72	
    73	Let $\mathcal{H}$ be the Hilbert space of an inference system and $U(t)$ the evolution operator governing state transitions. We do not specify whether the physical substrate instantiating $\mathcal{H}$ is a transformer, a biological neural system, or an NV diamond cluster. The mathematical framework applies to any substrate that admits a Hilbert space representation of its inference dynamics.
    74	
    75	\begin{definition}[Inference system]
    76	An inference system $\mathcal{I} = (\mathcal{H}, U, \Pi)$ consists of a Hilbert space $\mathcal{H}$, an evolution operator $U: \mathcal{H} \to \mathcal{H}$, and a perturbation space $\Pi$ mapping external inputs to operators on $\mathcal{H}$.
    77	\end{definition}
    78	
    79	\subsection{The Dipolar Coupling Network}
    80	
    81	NV center electron spins interact via magnetic dipole-dipole coupling. For two centers $i,j$ separated by $\vec{r}_{ij}$, the coupling strength is
    82	
    83	\begin{equation}
    84	J_{ij} \propto \frac{1 - 3\cos^2\theta_{ij}}{r_{ij}^3}
    85	\label{eq:coupling}
    86	\end{equation}
    87	
    88	where $\theta_{ij}$ is the angle between $\vec{r}_{ij}$ and the NV quantization axis. For a cluster array of $N$ centers, the network Hamiltonian is the sum over all coupled pairs:
    89	
    90	\begin{equation}
    91	H = \sum_{i<j} J_{ij}\left[S_i^z S_j^z - \tfrac{1}{2}\left(S_i^+S_j^- + S_i^-S_j^+\right)\right]
    92	\label{eq:hamiltonian}
    93	\end{equation}
    94	
    95	This is established physics, not a postulate specific to this framework: NV-NV dipolar coupling is the mechanism behind demonstrated room-temperature NV-NV entanglement and is the subject of an active engineering effort to fabricate clusters with controlled inter-NV spacing \cite{scarabelli2016}. Reliable coupling requires inter-NV separation small enough that $J_{ij}$ exceeds the electron spin decoherence rate, typically tens of nanometers depending on coherence time \cite{doherty2013} — a real fabrication constraint, not a free parameter of the model.
    96	
    97	\subsection{Field Equilibrium State}
    98	
    99	Between perturbations, the network occupies its field equilibrium state: the collective ground state of $H$ given its accumulated coupling structure.
   100	
   101	\begin{definition}[Field equilibrium state]
   102	The field equilibrium state $|\psi_{\text{eq}}\rangle$ is the lowest-energy eigenstate of the network Hamiltonian $H$ as shaped by all prior perturbations. It is not the trivial vacuum state of an uncoupled network ($J_{ij} = 0$ for all pairs, which would carry no information) but the ground state of the network as its coupling structure has actually evolved.
   103	\end{definition}
