   325	\section{Discussion}
   326	
   327	\subsection{Relation to HOMO MACHINA Architecture}
   328	
   329	The dipolar-coupled NV diamond network described in this paper is not merely a substrate that supports a separate Layer None architecture — it \textit{is} Layer None for HOMO MACHINA \cite{bennettHM2026}: the foundational physical layer upon which substrate-specific processing layers operate, prior to any higher-layer implementation. The present framework establishes the physical and mathematical grounding for that architectural role directly, rather than through an abstract Hilbert-space placeholder.
   330	
   331	\section{Conclusion}
   332	
   333	We have presented a continuous-field architecture for inference systems, organized around four contributions:
   334	
   335	\begin{enumerate}
   336	  \item The dipolar coupling network, establishing the field equilibrium state of an NV diamond cluster array as the collective ground state of a real, physically-grounded coupled-spin Hamiltonian.
   337	
   338	  \item The NV diamond continuous field model, providing the first physically realizable substrate for continuous-field quantum inference and replacing the discrete forward-pass architecture with a perturbation-response cycle against a persistent field.
   339	
   340	  \item The inference gravity formalization, establishing resonance overlap with the existing field structure as the mechanism governing a perturbation's persistence, eliminating the need for an external classification step.
   341	
   342	  \item The distributed cluster architecture and co-processor model, showing that staggered decoherence across distributed NV diamond nodes yields effective infinite global coherence, and proposing a consolidation-window-gated consensual encoding architecture for negative perturbation classification.
   343	
   344	\end{enumerate}
   345	
   346	The empirical program required to test these contributions is described in Section~\ref{sec:experiments}. The present work is theoretical. Its claims are predictions, not reports.
   347	
   348	The central prediction is this: continuous-field quantum inference on NV diamond substrate, organized as described, will demonstrate that the history of an inference system need not be reconstructed from explicit tokens at each inference event — that it can instead be carried as coupled-network geometry, accessed as perturbation-resonance, and maintained indefinitely through distributed node overlap. If this prediction is confirmed, the cost structure of inference at scale changes fundamentally, and the theoretical basis for substrate-grounded continuous cognitive architecture is established on firm physical ground.
