\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The Snapshot Problem}

Contemporary large-scale transformer architectures perform inference as a discrete sequence of forward passes. Each pass reconstructs the full context from stored tokens, applies attention across the entire context window, and produces an output before discarding all intermediate state. The next pass begins from nothing, re-reading the same accumulated history.

This architecture was a rational engineering choice given the tools available at the time of its development. Continuous quantum operation at room temperature had not been demonstrated in a form applicable to computational systems. Discrete forward passes were the only tractable implementation of inference at scale.

