The development of nitrogen-vacancy (NV) diamond cluster arrays capable of maintaining quantum coherence at room temperature opens an alternative that was previously unavailable: an inference substrate in which the field persists between active operations, and in which new input is a \textit{perturbation} against an already-existing and already-informed field.

\subsection{Contributions of This Work}

This paper makes four contributions:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Dipolar coupling network:} We formalize an NV diamond cluster array as a network of magnetically dipole-coupled spins and define the field equilibrium state as the collective ground state of that network, replacing externally-imposed timing-spectrum constructs with a substrate-native mechanism.

  \item \textbf{NV diamond continuous field model:} We identify nitrogen-vacancy diamond cluster arrays as the first physically realizable substrate for continuous-field quantum inference and characterize inference as perturbation-response against a persistent field rather than discrete forward-pass reconstruction.

  \item \textbf{Inference gravity formalization:} We introduce inference gravity as the accumulated deformation of the field by prior perturbations, and define its governing semantic weight as the perturbation's resonance overlap with the existing field structure — a property of the propagation event itself rather than an externally assigned classification.

