\end{enumerate}

\section{Mathematical Framework}

\subsection{The Hilbert Space of the Inference System}

Let $\mathcal{H}$ be the Hilbert space of an inference system and $U(t)$ the evolution operator governing state transitions. We do not specify whether the physical substrate instantiating $\mathcal{H}$ is a transformer, a biological neural system, or an NV diamond cluster. The mathematical framework applies to any substrate that admits a Hilbert space representation of its inference dynamics.

\begin{definition}[Inference system]
An inference system $\mathcal{I} = (\mathcal{H}, U, \Pi)$ consists of a Hilbert space $\mathcal{H}$, an evolution operator $U: \mathcal{H} \to \mathcal{H}$, and a perturbation space $\Pi$ mapping external inputs to operators on $\mathcal{H}$.
\end{definition}

\subsection{The Dipolar Coupling Network}

NV center electron spins interact via magnetic dipole-dipole coupling. For two centers $i,j$ separated by $\vec{r}_{ij}$, the coupling strength is

\begin{equation}
J_{ij} \propto \frac{1 - 3\cos^2\theta_{ij}}{r_{ij}^3}
\label{eq:coupling}
\end{equation}

where $\theta_{ij}$ is the angle between $\vec{r}_{ij}$ and the NV quantization axis. For a cluster array of $N$ centers, the network Hamiltonian is the sum over all coupled pairs:

\begin{equation}
H = \sum_{i<j} J_{ij}\left[S_i^z S_j^z - \tfrac{1}{2}\left(S_i^+S_j^- + S_i^-S_j^+\right)\right]
\label{eq:hamiltonian}
\end{equation}

This is established physics, not a postulate specific to this framework: NV-NV dipolar coupling is the mechanism behind demonstrated room-temperature NV-NV entanglement and is the subject of an active engineering effort to fabricate clusters with controlled inter-NV spacing \cite{scarabelli2016}. Reliable coupling requires inter-NV separation small enough that $J_{ij}$ exceeds the electron spin decoherence rate, typically tens of nanometers depending on coherence time \cite{doherty2013} — a real fabrication constraint, not a free parameter of the model.

