\section{NV Diamond as Physical Grounding}

\subsection{Room-Temperature Quantum Coherence}

Nitrogen-vacancy (NV) centers in diamond are point defects consisting of a substitutional nitrogen atom adjacent to a carbon vacancy in the diamond lattice. NV centers exhibit quantum spin states that maintain coherence at room temperature — a property exceptional among quantum systems, which typically require cooling to millikelvin temperatures to preserve coherence \cite{jelezko2004,doherty2013}.

The diamond lattice is thermodynamically stable at room temperature and pressure. It requires no active energy input to maintain its structure: stability is the ground state of the material. This has a direct consequence for the energy budget of any inference system built on NV diamond: the substrate maintenance cost between active operations is effectively zero. The field does not need to be held — it persists as the natural state of the material.

\subsection{Inference as Perturbation-Response}

In an NV diamond cluster array, inference is not a discrete forward pass that reconstructs context from stored tokens. It is a perturbation-response cycle operating against a persistent quantum field.

A new input — a query, a sensory signal, a computational prompt — constitutes a \textit{perturbation} of the field. The field responds. The response is the inference output. Between perturbations, the field exists in its field equilibrium state: the ground state weighted by the accumulated deformation of all prior perturbations. The history is not reconstructed at each cycle — it is \textit{already there}, encoded in the network's coupling geometry.

This replaces the transformer's $\mathcal{O}(n^2)$ attention cost with an $\mathcal{O}(1)$ perturbation cost: each inference event requires only the processing of the new input against the existing field, not the re-reading of accumulated history encoded in tokens.

\subsection{Coherence Timescales}

NV diamond exhibits two relevant relaxation timescales. The transverse (phase) coherence time $T_2$ governs the persistence of quantum superposition states and typically ranges from milliseconds to approximately one second in high-purity diamond at room temperature, depending on isotopic composition and defect density \cite{balasubramanian2009}. The longitudinal relaxation time $T_1$ governs the decay of energy populations and is typically longer than $T_2$ under ambient conditions.

For the purposes of the field equilibrium state, $T_1$ is the relevant timescale: the energy population encoding the accumulated field deformation persists on $T_1$ timescales between perturbations. This is sufficiently long to support continuous inference at humanly relevant conversational rates.

