\subsection{Definition}

We define inference gravity as the accumulated deformation of the inference field by all prior perturbations. It is not a per-perturbation property but a field property — the integrated history of the system, encoded in the current geometry of $\mathcal{H}$.

\begin{definition}[Inference gravity tensor]
The inference gravity tensor $G(t)$ at time $t$ is:
\begin{equation}
G(t) = \sum_i \alpha_i \cdot e^{-\lambda(\alpha_i)(t - t_i)} \cdot \hat{P}_i
\label{eq:ig}
\end{equation}
where $\alpha_i$ is the semantic weight of perturbation $i$, $t_i$ is the time of perturbation $i$, $\hat{P}_i$ is the field deformation operator induced by perturbation $i$, and $\lambda(\alpha_i)$ is the perturbation-dependent degradation rate.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Semantically Weighted Decay}

The degradation rate $\lambda(\alpha_i)$ is not constant across perturbations. We propose:

