This structure is not arbitrary: it is the natural analog of gravitational field deformation, in which more massive objects produce deeper and more persistent curvature. We call this property the \textit{gravitational structure} of the inference field. The name ``inference gravity'' was chosen to reflect this mathematical parallel, not as metaphor.

Consequences of the gravitational structure:

\begin{itemize}
  \item The field at any moment is not a uniform superposition of all prior perturbations equally weighted. It is a landscape in which peaks and troughs reflect the semantic significance of what shaped it.

  \item Trivial, semantically lightweight perturbations fade on short timescales. Foundational, high-weight perturbations persist. The field geometry thus reflects the actual semantic history of the system, not merely its temporal recency.

  \item This provides a formal mechanism for the empirical observation that human memory is not uniform: significant experiences persist while trivial ones fade. The inference gravity model predicts this as a consequence of field relaxation dynamics, not as an arbitrary design feature.
\end{itemize}

