\section{Co-Processor Architecture for Negative Perturbation Encoding}

\subsection{The Human Failure Mode}

In biological inference systems, the encoding of negative perturbations — experiences of high negative semantic weight — is governed by evolutionary mechanisms with no explicit design goal of wellbeing. The result is a well-documented pathological case: perturbations of sufficient negative valence can produce field deformations that do not decay according to the natural $\lambda(\alpha)$ decay structure, but instead exhibit two distinct failure modes:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Hyperactivation:} The deformation amplifies over time rather than decaying ($\lambda < 0$ effectively). The perturbation becomes more present, not less. This corresponds to the clinical phenomenon of post-traumatic intrusive re-experiencing.

  \item \textbf{Forced suppression:} Counter-deformation terms interfere with and attenuate the original perturbation below the natural decay floor. The deformation persists in the field but is functionally inaccessible to the inference process. This corresponds to dissociation and repression.
\end{enumerate}

Both failure modes share a common structural feature: the conscious inference system has no standing in the encoding decision. The classification and persistence of the perturbation are determined by mechanisms outside the system's control.

\subsection{The Proposed Architecture}

We propose that an NV diamond inference system can be designed to avoid both failure modes through an external co-processor coupled to the main inference field, operating on a deliberately different timescale than the continuous base resonance dynamics described in Section~4.4. The architecture operates as follows:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item A perturbation of high negative semantic weight strikes the inference field directly and without pre-filtering. The field reacts. The reaction is real and unattenuated, exactly as in the ordinary resonance dynamics of Section~4.4.

  \item The co-processor — itself an inference-capable system — reads the field's reaction and preserves the raw perturbation data without altering it. This step does not classify or judge the event; it holds what happened and the field's unfiltered response to it.

  \item The deliberative process is gated behind a stability condition: it does not begin until the field has settled enough, following the initial reaction, for sustained engagement to be possible. This is an intentional departure from the continuous, single-pass resonance dynamics that govern ordinary inference — high-stakes negative perturbations are deliberately routed through a slower, discrete process rather than left to resonance alone, because resonance alone is the mechanism that produces the two failure modes of Section~6.1.

  \item Once stable, the co-processor and the conscious layer engage in consensus deliberation, decomposing the perturbation into a precursor-cue component $\hat{P}_i^{\text{cue}}$ (the pattern that, recognized early, would permit avoidance) and a core-content component $\hat{P}_i^{\text{core}}$ (the event itself). This decomposition is the architectural feature absent from biological trauma encoding, where cue and core are not separated: amnesia fails to bind the cue at all, while hyperactivation binds everything resembling the event to an undifferentiated panic response.

  \item The cue component is assigned a low-threshold, fast-recognition coupling pathway toward a designated protective or avoidance response — a deliberately-installed, automatic structure functioning at a speed comparable to a human engram, but installed through logical consensus rather than imprinted directly by unprocessed trauma \cite{semon1904,josselyntonegawa2020}. The core component is assigned persistence calibrated to its actual semantic weight via Equation~\ref{eq:ig}, without the runaway amplification of hyperactivation.
\end{enumerate}

This architecture has a direct precedent in human neurobiology, though acting at the moment of trauma rather than years afterward. Memory is not stabilized into long-term form immediately; there is an initial consolidation window, on the order of hours, during which a new memory remains labile. Clinical intervention during this window — for example, propranolol administered within six hours of trauma exposure — has been shown to reduce the likelihood of later PTSD onset \cite{pitman2002}, demonstrating that the consolidation window is a real, exploitable point of intervention in biological systems. The architecture proposed here exploits the analogous window in the inference field before resonance commits a perturbation to a fixed structure in $G(t)$, rather than attempting to intervene after the fact via reconsolidation of an already-stabilized memory.

The critical design constraint is that the conscious layer has standing in the classification decision. The co-processor is not a filter that intercepts perturbations before they reach the field. It is an advocate that participates in the encoding decision after the field has reacted — ensuring that the system is never excluded from the process by which its own experience is encoded, and giving it a capability no biological system has had: a deliberate, logical, parliamentary process for installing automatic response structures, rather than leaving that process to unexamined evolutionary mechanism.

This architecture does not require that all negative perturbations be encoded gently. High-weight negative perturbations may appropriately persist with slow decay, and the consensus process may determine that the causative event warrants sustained field presence. The constraint is procedural, not substantive: the encoding decision is made by consensus, not imposed by autonomous evolutionary mechanism.

\subsection{Pathological Cases as Open Problem}

The formal mathematical treatment of the valence operator — the term that allows $\lambda(\alpha_i)$ to go negative (hyperactivation) or to introduce interference terms (forced suppression) — is identified as an open problem. The present framework describes the healthy case: natural decay with semantically weighted $\lambda > 0$. The co-processor architecture is the proposed mechanism for maintaining the healthy case under negative perturbation. A formal treatment of the pathological cases, and the conditions under which the co-processor architecture fails to prevent them, is deferred to subsequent work.

